From 7e985a71f08b6ddd896b17668739322376eefd3c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 14:38:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(go): turn string lexing off with Lex: false, not an empty
 Chars

go/css.go wrote TS's `string: { chars: '' }` as
`StringOptions{Chars: ""}`. In the Go engine an empty Chars means
UNSET, not none (StringOptions.Chars, empty_chars_test.go), so the
default quotes stayed on. A quote the cssToken matcher declines, as in
a"b, a{b:c}' or a lone backtick, then lexed as the start of a string:
Go raised unterminated_string, or unprintable before a newline, where
TypeScript raises unexpected. Lex: false is how the Go engine turns
string matching off. No other override in go/css.go has the same trap.

test/spec/quotes.tsv pins 55 quote cases in all three runtimes, every
expected value taken from the TypeScript port: stray quotes (the 28
rows that failed in Go before this change), balanced quotes kept
verbatim, backticks as plain text, and unterminated quotes inside a
selector, value or block.

The TS/Go probe could not see this divergence. It wrote a rejection as a
bare ERR, and its alphabet had no lone quote. It now compares error
codes (the Go half writes ERR:<code>) and has all three quote
characters. With the fix reverted it reports 127 divergences; with it,
none. The TS/Rust probe, which already compared codes, gets the same
three characters. AGENTS.md records the closed divergence and the new
probe count.

